### Define a Tool Handler Function with rmcp's `tool` Macro

Source: https://docs.rs/rmcp/latest/rmcp/attr.tool.html

Use this macro to mark an async function as a tool handler. It allows specifying the tool's name, description, and input schema. The function's documentation serves as its description if not explicitly provided. Ensure the `macros` and `server` crate features are enabled.

```rust
#[tool(name = "my_tool", description = "This is my tool", annotations(title = "我的工具", read_only_hint = true))]
pub async fn my_tool(param: Parameters<MyToolParam>) {
    // handling tool request
}
```

--------------------------------

### Create Transport from Standard IO (Client)

Source: https://docs.rs/rmcp/latest/rmcp/transport/index.html

This example shows how to create a client transport using standard input and output streams. It requires the `client` feature. The code sets up a client using stdin and stdout, then lists available tools.

```rust
// create transport from std io
#[cfg(feature = "client")]
async fn io()  -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let client = ().serve((tokio::io::stdin(), tokio::io::stdout())).await?;
    let tools = client.peer().list_tools(Default::default()).await?;
    println!("{:?}", tools);
    Ok(())
}
```

--------------------------------

### Create Stdin and Stdout

Source: https://docs.rs/rmcp/latest/rmcp/transport/io/fn.stdio.html

Available on crate feature `transport-io` and (crate features `client` or `server`) only. Use this function to get standard input and output streams.

```rust
pub fn stdio() -> (Stdin, Stdout)
```

--------------------------------

### Define a Simple Tool with Macros

Source: https://docs.rs/rmcp/latest/rmcp/handler/server/router/tool/index.html

Use `tool_router` and `tool` macros to define tools for MCP servers. This is suitable for small servers with simple interfaces. Ensure the `server` feature flag is enabled.

```rust
struct Server;

#[derive(Deserialize, schemars::JsonSchema, Default)]
struct AddParameter {
    left: usize,
    right: usize
}
#[derive(Serialize, schemars::JsonSchema)]
struct AddOutput {
    sum: usize
}
#[tool_router(server_handler)]
impl Server {
    #[tool(name = "adder", description = "Modular add two integers")]
    fn add(
        &self,
        Parameters(AddParameter { left, right }): Parameters<AddParameter>
    ) -> Json<AddOutput> {
        Json(AddOutput { sum: left.wrapping_add(right) })
    }
}
```

### rmcp Crate > Transports

Source: https://docs.rs/rmcp/latest/rmcp/index.html?search=std%3A%3Avec

The transport layer in rmcp is pluggable. Two built-in pairs are available: stdio (TokioChildProcess client, stdio server) and Streamable HTTP (StreamableHttpClientTransport client, StreamableHttpService server). Any type implementing the Transport trait can be used, with the IntoTransport helper trait providing automatic conversions from various stream and reader/writer types, worker implementations, or Transport implementations directly.

